"""Project-to-research map: overlapping forms, methods and philosophical lenses."""
from bs4 import BeautifulSoup
from html import escape
from urllib.parse import quote

ROWS = [
 ('Readers follow links between fragments.','Hypertext / hypertext fiction','Form / genre','hypertext fiction reader agency nonlinear narrative','Narratology + literary studies + interface design','Who chooses the order, and how does that shape meaning?'),
 ('Readers choose actions or enter commands that affect a story.','Interactive fiction / literary game','Overlapping genres','interactive fiction agency literary games choice','Game studies + literature + philosophy of action','What makes a choice meaningful rather than merely available?'),
 ('Code combines words or produces changing texts.','Generative poetry / generative literature','Genre / computational practice','generative poetry computational creativity authorship','Poetics + software studies + philosophy of authorship','Who or what contributes to the text: author, rules, data, reader?'),
 ('Words move, change over time or respond to interaction.','Digital poetry / kinetic typography','Genre + expressive technique','digital poetry kinetic typography temporality','Poetics + visual art + phenomenology','How do timing and movement change the experience of reading?'),
 ('A place or physical route shapes the experience.','Locative media / locative narrative','Medium / narrative practice','locative narrative place memory spatial storytelling','Geography + cultural memory + phenomenology','How do bodies, places and memories shape what can be experienced?'),
 ('People perform or write a story together through a network.','Netprov / networked narrative','Genre / participatory practice','netprov networked narrative collaborative authorship','Performance studies + sociology + literary studies','How is responsibility shared between participants and platforms?'),
 ('The story develops through several platforms or media.','Transmediality / transmedia storytelling','Cross-media approach','transmedia storytelling worldbuilding participation','Media studies + adaptation studies + narrative theory','What does each medium contribute that the others do not?'),
 ('Text, images, sound and choices tell the story together.','Visual novel / multimodal narrative','Genre + broader analytical category','visual novel multimodal narrative branching narrative','Game studies + visual culture + literary studies','How do image, language and sound distribute perspective?')]

ROWS += [
 ('People play through challenges, puzzles, movement or a simulated world.','Digital games: puzzle, platform, simulation and other game genres','Broad family / subgenres','game studies puzzle games platform games simulation player experience','Game studies + design + psychology','What do the rules let someone do, and what do those rules value?'),
 ('The image, artwork or visual experiment is the main experience.','Digital visual art / generative art','Art practices','digital art generative art computational aesthetics','Art history + software studies + aesthetics','How does the process of making an image affect its meaning?'),
 ('The work depends on the internet, a browser or online connections.','Internet art / browser art','Art practices','internet art browser art network aesthetics','Art history + media studies + platform studies','How does the network become part of the artwork?'),
 ('People watch moving images, a short film or an argument made through video.','Digital video / animation / video essay','Formats / practices','digital video animation video essay audiovisual essay','Film studies + visual culture + rhetoric','How do editing, image and sound make an argument or tell a story?'),
 ('People explore real events, interviews or documentary material.','Interactive documentary / digital documentary','Documentary forms','interactive documentary documentary ethics co-creation','Documentary studies + history + journalism','Whose account is presented, and how can the audience judge its evidence?'),
 ('People listen to a story, conversation, soundscape or sound artwork.','Podcast / audio narrative / sound art','Formats / art practices','podcast audio narrative sound art listening','Sound studies + media studies + musicology','How does listening create a sense of place or another person?'),
 ('People read or explore a chart, dataset, map or data story.','Data visualisation / data storytelling','Communication / analytical practices','data visualisation data storytelling critical data studies','Design + statistics + geography + ethics','What does the visualisation show, and what does it leave out?'),
 ('Posts, memes, streams or online communities are central.','Social media storytelling / memes / livestreaming','Platform-based cultural forms','social media storytelling meme studies livestreaming participatory culture','Media studies + sociology + cultural studies','Who can participate, and how does the platform shape what is seen?'),
 ('People rewrite, remix or expand an existing fictional world.','Fan fiction / remix / participatory culture','Genres / cultural practices','fan fiction remix transformative works fandom','Literary studies + fan studies + copyright ethics','What changes when readers become creators?'),
 ('People enter and look around a virtual space.','Virtual reality / immersive experience','Medium / experiential form','virtual reality presence embodiment immersive narrative','Media studies + interaction design + phenomenology','What creates the feeling of being there?'),
 ('Digital material appears over a physical place or object.','Augmented reality / mixed reality','Technologies / media','augmented reality mixed reality place narrative','Design + geography + heritage studies','How do digital layers change the way a place is understood?'),
 ('Bodies, sensors, a performance or a physical installation matter.','Interactive installation / digital performance','Art practices','interactive installation digital performance embodied interaction','Performance studies + art history + human-computer interaction','How does bodily participation become part of the work?')]

def atlas(root):
    labs = root / 'labs'
    path = labs / 'academic-library.html'
    s = BeautifulSoup(path.read_text(encoding='utf-8'),'html.parser')
    s.select_one('.al-library')['id'] = 'steps'
    for old in s.select('#genre-atlas, #across-fields'): old.decompose()
    html = '''<section class="al-atlas" id="genre-atlas"><p class="al-tag">WHAT KIND OF PROJECT AM I MAKING?</p><h2>Find words for your work.</h2><p>Start with what the reader or player actually does. Use the table to find possible terms, then search the research. You can belong to several rows at once. A tool such as Construct is not a genre.</p><p>This is a teaching map inspired by the <a href="https://glossary.cdn.uib.no/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Living Glossary of Digital Narrative</a>, not a fixed taxonomy. It brings genres, techniques and approaches together while keeping their differences visible.</p><label for="al-genre-query">Find a form, action or question<input id="al-genre-query" type="search" placeholder="Try choices, memory, poetry or authorship"/></label><p id="al-genre-count" role="status">8 routes into research</p><div class="al-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Digital forms and research routes" tabindex="0"><table><caption>From your project to a research question</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">What happens in my project?</th><th scope="col">Possible terms / type</th><th scope="col">Search for research</th><th scope="col">Fields to combine</th><th scope="col">A philosophical question to try</th></tr></thead><tbody>'''
    for clue,term,kind,keywords,fields,question in ROWS:
        query='https://oria.no/?query=any,contains,'+quote(keywords)
        html += f'<tr data-genre-row><td>{escape(clue)}</td><th scope="row">{escape(term)}<small>{escape(kind)}</small></th><td><code>{escape(keywords)}</code><p><a href="{escape(query)}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Search Oria ↗</a></p></td><td>{escape(fields)}</td><td>{escape(question)}</td></tr>'
    html += '''</tbody></table></div><p>The field combinations and questions are suggestions for your analysis, not definitions taken from the glossary. Compare the glossary entry with scholarship before deciding how to describe your work.</p><p><a href="https://elmcip.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ELMCIP: find creative and critical works</a> · <a href="https://glossary.cdn.uib.no/terms/transmediality/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read about transmediality in the glossary</a></p></section>'''
    html = html.replace('8 routes into research', f'{len(ROWS)} routes into research')
    html = html.replace('Find words for your work.', 'What are you making? Find a useful name.')
    html = html.replace('This is a teaching map inspired by the', 'This is a starting map of digital culture, not a complete list of everything people make. The digital narrative terms draw on the')
    html = html.replace('not a fixed taxonomy.', 'not one official classification system.')
    s.select_one('.al-controls').insert_before(BeautifulSoup(html,'html.parser').find())
    cross = '''<section class="al-reading" id="across-fields"><p class="al-tag">WORKING ACROSS FIELDS</p><h2>Combine reading lists. Make the connection precise.</h2><p>Your project does not have to sit inside one subject. A map story can draw on literature, geography and philosophy. A game can connect ethics, history and visual art. Reading across fields becomes useful when you can show what each source helps you understand.</p><ol><li><strong>Choose a form:</strong> what does someone do in your project? Find one or two terms in the table.</li><li><strong>Choose a question:</strong> memory, identity, agency, power, place, knowledge or responsibility.</li><li><strong>Combine sources:</strong> take one source about the form, one about your question and, if useful, one from another course's reading list. Check that each source actually fits.</li><li><strong>Connect the argument:</strong> explain where the sources support, complicate or disagree with each other. Keep their different meanings of a term visible.</li></ol><h3>Example: a campus story about belonging</h3><p>Describe it as a locative or hypertext narrative if those terms fit the work. Use narrative theory to discuss the reader's route, cultural geography to investigate place, and philosophy to ask whose experience is recognised. Search combinations such as <code>locative narrative belonging</code> and <code>place memory phenomenology</code>.</p><p>This is an example research plan, not an assigned interpretation. Read the sources before making the claim.</p><h3>Philosophy is a way to sharpen the question.</h3><p>Agency asks what it means to act or choose. Epistemology asks what counts as knowledge and evidence. Ethics asks about responsibility and consequences. Phenomenology examines experience. Philosophy of technology investigates how technologies shape human life and practice.</p><p><a href="https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/technology/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy: Philosophy of Technology</a> · <a href="https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-computer/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Computer and Information Ethics</a></p><p><strong>Transmedial is not the same as interdisciplinary.</strong> A story can unfold across media; a study can combine academic fields. Your project can do both. Several media on one page do not automatically make a transmedia story: ask how each part contributes to the story.</p><p><a href="#genre-atlas">Return to the genre table ↑</a> · <a href="#reading">How to read and cite your sources ↓</a></p></section>'''
    fields = BeautifulSoup(cross,'html.parser').find()
    fields.select_one('ol').insert_before(BeautifulSoup('''<section class="al-pride"><p class="al-tag">FROM INGRID'S OWN PROJECT / DIKULT216</p><h3>Work, study and something you care about.</h3><blockquote>When I worked for Bergen Pride, I made a Pride Park game in DIKULT216. I could combine work and university, and explore what I care about: belonging and inclusion.</blockquote><p class="al-attribution">Ingrid Ormevik</p><p>Your experience can be a starting point for academic work. For a project like this, you could combine game studies with queer studies, cultural studies and ethics. That is a possible research route, not a claim about the sources used in Ingrid's project.</p><p><strong>Try the connection:</strong> game design + belonging + inclusion. Ask who feels welcome, what makes participation possible and how the rules express those choices.</p><p>Search for <code>queer game studies belonging</code>, <code>inclusive game design</code> or <code>participation ethics</code>. Use relevant readings from different courses, then explain what each contributes.</p></section>''','html.parser').find())
    s.select_one('#reading').insert_before(fields)
    s.select_one('.al-library h1').insert_before(BeautifulSoup('<nav aria-label="Library sections"><a href="#genre-atlas">01 / Find my genre</a><a href="#across-fields">02 / Connect fields</a><a href="#sources">03 / Find sources</a><a href="#reading">04 / Read and cite</a></nav>','html.parser').find())
    for old in s.select('link[href^="genre-atlas.css"],script[src^="genre-atlas.js"]'): old.decompose()
    s.head.append(BeautifulSoup('<link rel="stylesheet" href="genre-atlas.css?v=2"/><script defer src="genre-atlas.js?v=1"></script>','html.parser'))
    for link in s.select('link[href^="academic-library.css"]'):
        link['href'] = 'academic-library.css?v=library-3'
    article = s.select_one('.al-library')
    hero = s.new_tag('header', attrs={'class':'al-hero'})
    for child in list(article.children):
        if getattr(child,'attrs',{}).get('id') == 'genre-atlas': break
        hero.append(child.extract())
    article.insert(0,hero)
    path.write_text(str(s),encoding='utf-8',newline='\n')
